{
 "lead": "Some waits are sums of smaller waits; some lifetimes wear out; some quantities grow by multiplying many small factors. Gamma, Weibull and log-normal distributions model these three positive, right-skewed situations. Gamma is for the time to the k-th event in a Poisson process. Weibull is for component life and failure rates that change with age. Log-normal is for positive data whose logarithm is normal, such as incomes, claim sizes and prices. All three appear in reliability, insurance, hydrology and finance. Gamma and Weibull are defined for t ≥ 0: at t = 0 their density may be zero, finite or infinite, depending on the shape. Log-normal is defined only for t > 0.",
 "prereq": "You need continuous random variables and pdfs (Unit 2), the <a href=\"#exponential-distribution-and-the-memoryless-property\">exponential wait</a>, the <a href=\"#normal-distribution-and-its-properties\">normal curve</a>, and the gamma function Γ(·).",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Gamma distribution: sum of waits",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The gamma distribution describes the wait for the k-th event in a Poisson process with rate λ. The pdf is:"
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "f(t) = λ<sup>k</sup> t<sup>k−1</sup> e<sup>−λt</sup> / Γ(k),  t ≥ 0",
     "where": "k > 0 is the shape, λ > 0 is the rate, t is time, e is the base of natural logs, and Γ(k) = ∫₀<sup>∞</sup> u<sup>k−1</sup> e<sup>−u</sup> du is the gamma function."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The mean is k/λ and the variance is k/λ². With scale θ = 1/λ the pdf becomes f(t) = t<sup>k−1</sup> e<sup>−t/θ</sup> / (θ<sup>k</sup> Γ(k)) and the mean is kθ. When k = 1 the gamma is the exponential in Chapter 3.5. When k is a positive integer it is the sum of k independent exponential waits. Chi-square is a gamma special case; Unit 5 gives the details. Gamma is used for call-center waiting, insurance claim totals and rainfall totals."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Weibull distribution: lifetime and hazard",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Weibull models time to failure. Its pdf is:"
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "f(t) = (β/η)(t/η)<sup>β−1</sup> exp(−(t/η)<sup>β</sup>),  t ≥ 0",
     "where": "β > 0 is the shape, η > 0 is the scale or characteristic life, and exp means e raised to the power shown."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The mean is ηΓ(1 + 1/β). The survival function is S(t) = exp(−(t/η)<sup>β</sup>). The hazard rate is h(t) = (β/η)(t/η)<sup>β−1</sup>: the chance of failure just after t given survival to t. If β = 1, h = 1/η is constant and Weibull is exponential. If β > 1, hazard rises with age, which fits wear-out. If β < 1, hazard falls, which fits infant mortality. Reliability engineers use it for bulbs, bearings and wind speed. β is not a rate; η sets the time scale."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Log-normal distribution: multiplicative effects",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "X is log-normal when ln X is normal. Write Y = ln X ~ N(μ, σ²); then X = e<sup>Y</sup>. The pdf is:"
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "f(t) = 1/(t σ √(2π)) exp(−(ln t − μ)<sup>2</sup>/(2σ<sup>2</sup>)),  t > 0",
     "where": "μ and σ are the mean and standard deviation of ln X, not of X; ln is natural log and π is 3.14159."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The median is e<sup>μ</sup>. The mean is e<sup>μ+σ²/2</sup>, so mean > median when σ > 0 and the density is right-skewed. Log-normal is used for incomes, insurance claim sizes, stock prices, particle sizes and rainfall. To use the normal table, take logs first. It is not the log of a normal variable; it is the variable whose log is normal."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
